// Error recovery which runs and gives up, even though the grammar marks a place to resume at. `@error ";"` sits inside
// `body`, so the error symbol can only be shifted while a `body` is being parsed, which is between the parentheses.
// An error outside them is nowhere near that place: the recovery pops the stack looking for a state which shifts the
// error symbol, finds none, unwinds down to the start state, and the parse is over. The trace ends in FAIL rather than
// RESYNC, which is what says the recovery failed as opposed to the grammar carrying no `@error` at all.
//
// The input is a whole parenthesised body followed by a stray semicolon. The body parses cleanly, so the trace first
// shows a shift and a reduce for it; the semicolon then arrives where the start symbol is already complete and only the
// end of input is allowed. The recovery pops the reduced `program` and reaches the start state, which cannot shift the
// error symbol, so it fails. The pop in front of the FAIL is what shows the recovery tried.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are single characters and runs of digits.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    NUMBER:    /[0-9]+/;
    OPEN:      "(";
    CLOSE:     ")";
    SEMICOLON: ";";
}

@parser {
    program
        : "(" body ")"
        ;

    body
        : NUMBER
        | body NUMBER
        | @error ";"
        ;
}
